6. github.com/karanb192/claude-code-hooks and
   github.com/VoxCore84/claude-code-hook-tester.
   Both licensed under the MIT License.

   The testing method for this package's hook tier came from that
   repository: a zero-dependency runner, tests colocated with the plugin
   they cover, and an explicit stdin and stdout integration tier that feeds
   a real event payload into the real hook and asserts on the real output.
   The runner at tests/hooks/run.sh is this package's own bash, written
   against this package's own hook contract. A word-sequence comparison of
   everything under tests/ against that repository found no shared test
   text: the only matches are a node import line, a git config line inside a
   fixture, and the hook JSON key sequence the format requires. No code was
   copied, so its notice is not reproduced; the attribution is here and in
   the Sources list of docs/handbook/testing.md.

   The hook-tester repository is where the same tier gets its payload and its
   pass and fail contract: a mock event payload per lifecycle event sent on
   stdin, and the discipline of asserting a hook's real three-way outcome
   (0 passes, 2 is an intentional block, anything else is a crash) instead of
   reading any non-zero exit as a failure. This package's hook signals a block
   through the JSON decision field rather than through exit 2, so
   tests/hooks/run.sh applies that discipline to this protocol instead: exit 0
   with empty output is an allow, exit 0 carrying a deny decision is an
   intentional block, and a non-zero exit is a crash. Its one file is Python
   and this package's runner is bash; the same word-sequence comparison found
   no overlap at all with either that file or its payload fixtures, so its
   notice is not reproduced either. It is cited in docs/handbook/testing.md
   and in that chapter's Sources list.

